  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 247 AM EDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nCurrent-Wednesday...Weak frontal boundary will remain in proximity \nto central FL into early Wed, then lift back northward. Light \nonshore flow develops today with early formation of a diffuse sea \nbreeze and penetration well inland for a change. An eventual late \nday/evening collision should occur across WCFL today, and the \ncollision on Wed will also occur across the interior. PWATs remain\nvery high, well in excess of 2\". Steering flow will be weak and \nerratic today and much of the same into Wed. PoPs today 30-50% \nalong the coast and 50-70% into the interior - highest may be \nalong the Kissimmee River. Probably expecting highest PoPs on Wed\nacross the eastern/central FL peninsula with 50-70% PoPs \nareawide. Aloft, we have weak troughing thru mid-week aiding \nconvection allowing for continued higher chances. Primary storm \nimpacts remain frequent lightning strikes, gusty winds locally \n40-50 mph, and torrential downpours capable of 2-4\" in a \nrelatively short period (esp interior) promoting minor/nuisance \nflooding in repeated rounds. \n\nMax temps near climo in the U80s/L90s. Humid overnight with mins in \nthe 70s. Peak heat indices in the U90s to L100s ahead of daily \nstorms. \n\nThu-Mon...Subtle shortwave troughing aloft will traverse central FL \naiding mostly diurnal convection thru the period. At the surface, \nwith the former trough lifting north moisture will surge across the \nregion into the weekend. Weak ridging noses back toward and across \nthe central FL peninsula thru late work-week and points further \nnorth thru the weekend. The pressure pattern remains fairly weak and \nexpect daily sea breeze formation and push inland. PWAT values \nremain in excess of 2\" into Sun, then perhaps some slightly drier \nair late Sun into Mon. Generally light SW storm steering Thu-Sat \nbecoming light SERLY Sat night-Mon. Still expect occasional \nerratic storm motions surrounding stronger late day boundary \ncollisions. Fairly high PoP numbers (60-70%) continue. Primary \nstorm impacts continue to revolve around locally heavy rainfall. \nNear seasonal temps for highs (U80s-L90s) with lows in the 70s and\nconditions remaining humid. Peak heat indices continue in the \nU90s to M100s.",
